From f55258761019334b30c34c0d5e22c2f8201bdd5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Fairfield <tom@fairfieldt.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 11:11:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 0/2] hwmon: (applesmc) Mac Pro 6,1 port lights

The Mac Pro 6,1 lights the ports on its I/O panel for a few seconds when
it's moved: 20 white LEDs, driven by the SMC through three LED
controllers on its own I2C bus. Linux can't control them today. This
series registers them as LED class devices and exposes what the SMC
firmware does with them by itself.

Patch 1 adds the LED devices and the firmware's own behaviour: automatic
or manual mode, lighting on motion, the hold time, the two animations
(Hello and You Again) and the power light's modes, documented in
Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-devices-platform-applesmc.

Patch 2 adds port_lights_levels, which sets every LED in one write and
sends only the ones that changed. It's separate because it duplicates
the LED class path; it's for userspace that animates the panel many
times a second, such as the music visualizer at 40 frames a second I
wrote it for. If you'd rather not have it, patch 1 stands alone.

Nothing documents these keys. I took the SMC firmware image from Apple's
Mac Pro SMC Firmware Update 2.0 (2.20f18), found the key table (16-byte
entries: name, flags, length, type, and a pointer to a RAM variable or a
handler), disassembled the handlers that touch the LED controller state,
and checked each key on the hardware with a userspace SMC tool before
writing the driver. Two notes for anyone poking at the same SMC:

- The Lsb*, Lsm*, LsEB and LsEF keys belong to the LED controllers'
  firmware update and factory data. Writing LsEB=1 while probing switched
  the SMC's LED handler into its update mode (LsHS 4, controllers
  unreadable) until the machine was unplugged; the firmware was intact
  afterwards. The driver writes none of these.
- Selecting an animation with LIGO 8 or 9 also lights the panel, and
  nothing in the SMC switches back from You Again, which is why patch 1
  reselects Hello afterwards with LIEN briefly cleared.

How this was made: an AI coding assistant (Claude, claude-opus-5-5, in
Claude Code) did the firmware analysis and wrote the code, the
documentation and these messages over one long session at my direction.
I identified each LED by eye, watched the animations and the power light
modes, and tested on my Mac Pro. A second AI reviewer (Codex) reviewed an
earlier version and found three races, all fixed here: a failed bulk
write couldn't be retried, a queued single-LED update could land after a
later bulk write, and a port_lights_motion write could be undone by the
Hello switch-back.

Tested on a Mac Pro 6,1 (SMC 2.20f18, LED controller firmware 1.4.a.0.6)
running v7.3-rc7 with unrelated local patches, with the driver built as a
module and reloaded while a fan control service used its hwmon
attributes. Both patches build with W=1
and pass checkpatch --strict. drivers/hwmon/applesmc.c is the same in
v7.3 and hwmon-next.

Tom Fairfield (2):
  hwmon: (applesmc) Add support for the Mac Pro 6,1 port lights
  hwmon: (applesmc) Add port_lights_levels to set all port lights at
    once

